identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: "10.17605/OSF.IO/X5DCF"
    description: "Preregistered plan for the confirmatory pan-cancer analysis, registered before it was run"
  - type: doi
    value: "10.17605/OSF.IO/N8DW6"
    description: "Preregistered plan for the follow-up test of the post hoc relationship; no analysis covered by it is in this release"
abstract: >-
  R analysis code, preregistered decision rules, result files, run logs and
  figures for a study of what omitting tumor proliferation does to prognostic
  gene expression estimates. For every expressed gene in a cohort, two Cox models
  are fitted for overall survival, one adjusted for age, sex and stage and one
  that adds a proliferation score; the ratio of the two hazard ratios is
  regressed on the gene's correlation with that score. Discovery used TCGA-LIHC
  with replication in GSE14520. A confirmatory analysis across further cancer
  types was registered before it was run, and both of its hypotheses were
  refuted; the refutation and the structure within it are reported as such, with
  every exploratory and post hoc analysis labeled in the code and in the output.
  A survey of 50 recent prognostic gene expression papers, drawn under a fixed
  seed, records how often such studies adjust for proliferation at all.
  A post hoc analysis asks how much of the per-gene shift the first-order omitted
  variable expression accounts for; neither of the two readings fixed in advance
  holds, and the remainder is reported as unexplained by either account.
